Z||000002||Formula88||12-24-2001||10:34 PM||||That's the idea.  You can try to drill out the entire bolt and retap, or try another easy-out.  Definately soak the area with penetrating oil (i.e. Liquid Wrench) and/or use the torch to heat it up good.  <P>Good Luck!||||reg||4
Z||000003||avengador1||12-25-2001||10:37 AM||||Soak the area with a good penetrating oil like pb buster or kroil. Get a reverse rotation drill bit and drill it out for an easy out (if you're lucky it will unscrew as you drill it this way). Sears has them and another type of their own. They are called screww extractor sets.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by avengador1 (edited 12-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||85 gt fiero||12-25-2001||09:03 PM||||Make sure you wipe the pentatrating oil up before you put the torch on it or you might have some more serious problems <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Smoooooth GT||12-25-2001||09:34 PM||||Yeah.. What they said!!!  Good Luck Yo_... <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooooothgtsig_9.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BABIE_BUN_SHAKERS.gif">                       <BR>      <P><A HREF="http://www.thefibersmith.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thefibersmith.com</A>   <BR>Technical  Support (903) 825-7744 Steve<BR>"Smoooooth GT"<BR>Shaved door handles, sidetrim, exhaust outlet's, etc.... smooooth!!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||skitime||12-25-2001||09:36 PM||||Don't bother trying to drill it out. The tap is hardened steel.  The drill bit will never hold up to the hardened steel.  I taught metal shop to high school kids who broke taps as often a breaking out in zits. The best way to remove them is buy a high quality center punch and break the tap up into small pieces. Because it is hardened steel it is very brittle. (That is why is broke so easily in the first place). Break it into pieces and carefully remove the broken pieces till it is completely out. If you try to drill it out you will end up with a hole beside the original hole.  <P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Archie||12-25-2001||10:48 PM||||Exactly what Ski said......you're never going to drill out a Tap.<P>Archie<P>------------------<BR>Just how fast do you want to go?||||reg||1
Z||000008||SKIDMARK||12-26-2001||12:29 AM||||Which manifold? On the exhaust, if you have a cutting torch you can blow the broken tap out with that. Just heat the edge of the broken tap til it melts, then poor on the oxy right into the hole. If your quick and accurate about it, you won't heat up the surrounding area much at all. Protect adjacent parts from the molten metal and flame. This is best done with the manifold removed though. You don't want to take a chance of damaging good parts. Done this many times on exhaust manifold bolts. Just chase it with a tap afterwords to clean the slag out of the threads. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||avengador1||12-26-2001||06:34 PM||||What exactly did you break? Was it a tap? Or was it the stud? Im rereading the post and am confused as to what was broken. If it was a tap, you will never be able to drill it out unless you have carbide or diamond bit. I don't think they make those in drill bits only in mill bits. You might have to take the heads off and take them to a tool & die shop to have the broken tap EDM'ed out and the hole retapped.<p>[This message has been edited by avengador1 (edited 12-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||12-26-2001||08:58 PM||||the way I read it is he broke the EZ out or tap off in the hole. Good luck. broken exhaust studs have to be the single worse job to repair on Fiero. Id rather replace an engine than fix em, LOL.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Oreif||12-27-2001||02:32 AM||||Breaking an Easy-Out sucks. I won't use them again. Anyhow when I snapped one on a broken bolt, I just got 5 of the red cone shaped dremel tool grinding bits. I was able to gring out the hardened parts on about 20 minutes. Then you can drill and re-tap the hole. You will need 5 of them to get thru it. Don't get the blue ones, they seem to degrade faster and fall apart.||||reg||1
